[node name="Tiles" type="TabBar" parent="HBoxContainer/Sidebar/TabContainer/Guide/TabContainer"]
layout_mode = 2

[node name="RichTextLabel" type="RichTextLabel" parent="HBoxContainer/Sidebar/TabContainer/Guide/TabContainer/Tiles"]
layout_mode = 1
anchors_preset = 15
anchor_right = 1.0
anchor_bottom = 1.0
grow_horizontal = 2
grow_vertical = 2
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[b]Tile Types[/b]
Terminal: 1 or 9 of any suit.
Simple: 2 through 8 of any suit.
Honor: Dragons and Winds.
Yakuhai: Dragons and prevalant/sitting wind specifically.

[b]Tile Groupings[/b]
Pair: 2 tiles of the same value and suit.
Set: 3 tiles of the same value and suit.
Quad: 4 tiles of the same value and suit. Calling a quad reveals another quad indicator.

Run: 3 tiles in a row of the same suit.

[b]Tile Bonuses[/b]
Some tiles are worth as many points as additional hands, although simply possesing them does not constitute a valid hand. They are as follows:
Red: Printed as red.
Quad: Every time a quad indicator is revealed, the tiles following it in sequence becomes a bonus tile. For number tiles, the order is numeric (1...9>1). For dragons, the order is W>G>R>W. For Winds: N>E>S>W>N."

[node name="Waits" type="TabBar" parent="HBoxContainer/Sidebar/TabContainer/Guide/TabContainer"]
visible = false
layout_mode = 2

[node name="RichTextLabel" type="RichTextLabel" parent="HBoxContainer/Sidebar/TabContainer/Guide/TabContainer/Waits"]
layout_mode = 1
anchors_preset = 15
anchor_right = 1.0
anchor_bottom = 1.0
grow_horizontal = 2
grow_vertical = 2
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[b]Two-sided wait[/b]
Two adjacent tiles awaiting a third consecutive tile from either side to complete them as a run. E.g. [45] is a two-sided wait on 3 and 6.

[b]One-sided wait[/b]
As above, but with only one wait. E.g. [12], [46], or [88].

[b]Three-sided wait[/b]
As example, [223] waits for 1, a duplicate 2, or 3. [45678] waits for a 3, 6, or 9.

There are additional ways of waiting on even more tiles. These will be left as an excercise for the reader. Note that \"waits\" are largely conceptual, they're just a convenient way of thinking about the probabilities different hands have of being completed."

[node name="Score" type="TabBar" parent="HBoxContainer/Sidebar/TabContainer/Guide/TabContainer"]
visible = false
layout_mode = 2

[node name="RichTextLabel" type="RichTextLabel" parent="HBoxContainer/Sidebar/TabContainer/Guide/TabContainer/Score"]
layout_mode = 1
anchors_preset = 15
anchor_right = 1.0
anchor_bottom = 1.0
grow_horizontal = 2
grow_vertical = 2
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "The base value of a hand is defined by:
	fu×2^(2+han),
rounded up to the nearest 10. This is then multiplied by 6 if in the East seat, else by 4.

Han, aka \"doublers\", are the real determining factor in a score. They are the sum of han from yaku met and the amount of dora tiles in hand.

Base fu is 20, additional fu are determined by a number of factors, generally incentivizing risk. See the \"Fu\" tab.

This is approximately how real riichi calculates base points, but there's more rounding and multiplying stages there."
tab_size = 2

